# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-lifecycle-rules -- one launch/quit behaviour for every CAD, platform and mode
#+STATUS: IN-PROGRESS
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: enhancement
#+DEPENDS: alfe-owned-cad-termination-after-recorded-results
#+BRANCH: cad-shutdown-never-saves (MR-A), then MR-B..MR-E
#+DATE: 2026-11-09

* Decision (pjb, 2026-11-09)
After the launch/quit review of 2026-11-09, the same behaviour with every CAD
on every platform:

1. The shutdown code never saves a drawing (a script may save it itself):
   drawings are closed without saving, and QUIT is answered No (=_QUIT _N=).
2. The shutdown quits and/or kills a CAD process only if alfe started it.
   Batch always launches, then quits/kills. Automation either launches, then
   quits/kills, or connects, then disconnects -- after closing the documents
   the script opened (snapshot of the documents at the start).
   A script that ends with an error status: that status is reported. A
   script that succeeds: that shows in the logs, and a bad shutdown status
   (e.g. a kill because quit failed) may be reported only then; a CI job can
   read the test result from the log and ignore the bad shutdown.
3. A failed start: a CAD alfe launched is killed.
4. Control-C is forwarded through the protocol; when the script is busy and
   does not acknowledge it and quit, a second Control-C forces the shutdown
   (disconnect from, or kill, the launched CAD).
5. Startup is protected too: cleanup happens in every case.
6. The timeouts stay logical (follow-up message, same day): a startup
   timeout that covers the bridge's own waits, distinct from the per-request
   timeout; one shutdown sequence for every CAD.

This revises the 2026-11-06 contract on one point: a forced end after a
successful script was a diagnostic; it is now a bad shutdown status, reported
only when the script succeeded, alongside a log line giving the script's result.

* Plan
- [X] MR-A (2.3.121): rule 1, and rule 2's quit/disconnect -- AutoCAD batch
  no longer QSAVEs; =._QUIT _N= in every script; COM bridges close only the
  run's documents of an attached CAD (snapshot), restore its visibility and
  the EPURE profile (new =:before-close= slot), and disconnect; BricsCAD batch
  restores SECURELOAD; the dead AutoCAD quit path (quit-created-autocad,
  quit-autocad.vbs, TASKKILL by PID, com-flags reader) removed.
- [X] MR-B (2.3.122): rule 2's status precedence (%RUN-EXIT-STATUS: a failed
  script's status wins; a bad shutdown -- :cad-terminated, :owned-cad-forced,
  survivor, unidentified, restoration not acknowledged -- sets the status only
  after a successful script, with a stderr line saying so); rule 5 (the start
  inside the run's protection, %TEARDOWN-RUN never signals, the first error
  kept); rule 3 (END-CREATED-COM-CAD on a failed start, the identity captured
  before the readiness wait); one SHUTDOWN-CAD-SESSION for every CAD (batch
  always quits, -i included; a restoration not acknowledged still closes);
  FAILED ends the READY wait; timeouts: --ready-timeout /
  $AUTOLISP_READY_WAIT_SECS (60 batch, 300 automation, 420 EPURE, never less
  than --timeout), the bridges' waits inside it, 15 s stop acknowledgement,
  20 s exit grace, 10 s kill. The unimported backend-protocol-error is gone
  with the move of the shutdown code to cad-common.
- [X] MR-C (2.3.123): rule 4 -- %ON-CAD-INTERRUPT / %CALL-WITH-CAD-INTERRUPTS
  around the run and its teardown: the first Control-C sends INTERRUPT (the
  runtime restores, publishes INTERRUPTED, ends its loop) and the plan stops
  (130); a second one, or the first while starting, forces the shutdown
  (FORCED-CAD-SHUTDOWN: launched CAD killed, attached one disconnected).
  Bridge watchdogs: alfe's PID and the CAD's (attached CADs captured to
  attached-cad-process.json, never used to end them). The clautolisp
  subprocess --timeout is deferred to
  [[file:deferred-clautolisp-request-timeout.issue][deferred-clautolisp-request-timeout]]: it needs the same
  per-action bound in the direct variant (the parity contract).
- [X] MR-D (2.3.124): macOS BricsCAD automation -- the AppleScript launcher
  records the PID and start time of the BricsCAD it launched or attached to;
  the run watches that process (ALIVE-FN), not the exited osascript; a
  launched one quits itself after the session (*AUTOLISP_QUIT_AFTER_SESSION*)
  and END-LAUNCHED-PID terminates it past the grace, on a failed start or a
  forced shutdown -- only while the PID keeps its start time; an attached one
  is never signalled.
- [X] MR-E (docs): the user manual's "Engine lifecycle: launch,
  communication, termination" chapter -- an overview table per backend and
  mode, then launching or attaching, timeouts, a failed start, Control-C,
  closing drawings, the owned-COM cleanup, the exit status, a killed alfe --
  gathering the sections that were under "Selecting a specific CAD install"
  and after "See also"; the EPURE COM subsections under the EPURE plug-in;
  stale statements fixed (exit code 2 -> 64, "silently ignored", the 30 s
  default, the EPURE qualification verdict). Specification: one COM bridge
  contract replaces both verbatim bridges and their stale notes; BricsCAD
  Windows batch, AutoCAD macOS/Linux, the bash "Cleanup and signals", exit
  codes 2/3, AUTOLISP_BACKEND's second meaning, a dangling link. Man page:
  CAD CLEANUP.

